1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,578
On the evening of the twenty-fifth of
December, nineteen ninety-one, a red flag

2
00:00:05,628 --> 00:00:07,330
came down over the Kremlin.

3
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:13,218
For almost seven decades, that flag had
flown over one of the two most powerful

4
00:00:13,268 --> 00:00:14,378
nations on Earth.

5
00:00:14,428 --> 00:00:19,834
The Soviet Union stretched across eleven
time zones, held fifteen republics, and

6
00:00:19,884 --> 00:00:23,790
was home to roughly two hundred
and ninety million people.

7
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:29,260
It had sent the first satellite,
and the first human, into space.

8
00:00:29,310 --> 00:00:32,930
Its army faced the West
across a divided Europe.

9
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:39,442
And yet, within a few months, it ceased
to exist. No foreign army invaded.

10
00:00:39,492 --> 00:00:41,310
No bomb fell on Moscow.

11
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:48,367
So what happened? How does a superpower
end, not with a bang, but with a signature?

12
00:00:48,417 --> 00:00:52,080
This is the story of how
the Soviet Union fell:

13
00:00:52,130 --> 00:00:58,638
a stagnant economy, a costly war, a disaster
at Chernobyl, and a leader who set out

14
00:00:58,688 --> 00:01:04,010
to reform the system, and set in
motion forces he could not control.

15
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:09,880
To understand why the Soviet Union fell,
start with how strong it once looked.

16
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:15,594
It rebuilt itself after the Second World
War, built an atomic bomb, and in nineteen

17
00:01:15,644 --> 00:01:19,656
fifty-seven launched Sputnik,
the first satellite in orbit.

18
00:01:19,706 --> 00:01:24,750
In nineteen sixty-one, Yuri Gagarin
became the first human being in space.

19
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:29,543
Its rivalry with the United
States shaped the whole world.

20
00:01:29,593 --> 00:01:35,389
Both sides built thousands of nuclear
weapons, backed opposing governments from

21
00:01:35,439 --> 00:01:41,458
Cuba to Vietnam, and in nineteen sixty-two
came close to nuclear war over missiles

22
00:01:41,508 --> 00:01:42,050
in Cuba.

23
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:47,949
In Moscow, each May Day, columns of soldiers
and missiles rolled across Red Square.

24
00:01:47,999 --> 00:01:53,219
From nineteen sixty-four, the man on the
reviewing stand was Leonid Brezhnev, who

25
00:01:53,269 --> 00:01:56,211
sat down with American
presidents as an equal.

26
00:01:56,261 --> 00:02:00,830
To the outside world, the Soviet
Union looked like a permanent fixture.

27
00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:03,712
At home, something was wrong.

28
00:02:03,762 --> 00:02:08,888
The planned economy that had once
raced ahead was slowing down.

29
00:02:08,938 --> 00:02:15,460
Factories met their targets on paper,
shops ran short of basic goods, and people

30
00:02:15,510 --> 00:02:21,210
stood in long queues. Reform was
discussed, and then quietly put away.

31
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:26,217
Brezhnev's long rule came to be
called the era of stagnation.

32
00:02:26,267 --> 00:02:32,349
When he died in nineteen eighty-two, two
elderly successors followed, and both died

33
00:02:32,399 --> 00:02:34,048
within about two years.

34
00:02:34,098 --> 00:02:39,663
Then, in March nineteen eighty-five,
the Politburo chose a much younger man,

35
00:02:39,713 --> 00:02:42,470
fifty-four-year-old Mikhail Gorbachev.

36
00:02:43,020 --> 00:02:48,220
Gorbachev knew that the system was in
trouble. He did not want to destroy it;

37
00:02:48,270 --> 00:02:49,652
he wanted to save it.

38
00:02:49,702 --> 00:02:54,562
His plan came down to two Russian
words that the world would soon learn.

39
00:02:54,612 --> 00:02:56,744
Perestroika meant restructuring:

40
00:02:56,794 --> 00:03:02,335
loosening the grip of central planning,
and letting enterprises make some of their

41
00:03:02,385 --> 00:03:03,290
own decisions.

42
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:05,458
Glasnost meant openness.

43
00:03:05,508 --> 00:03:09,766
Newspapers began to report problems
that had long been hidden.

44
00:03:09,816 --> 00:03:11,712
Banned books were published.

45
00:03:11,762 --> 00:03:17,480
Citizens could criticise their leaders in
public for the first time in decades, and

46
00:03:17,530 --> 00:03:20,190
many political prisoners were released.

47
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:24,282
Abroad, Gorbachev changed
the tone just as much.

48
00:03:24,332 --> 00:03:29,745
He met Ronald Reagan in Geneva and in
Reykjavik, and in December nineteen

49
00:03:29,795 --> 00:03:35,508
eighty-seven they signed the INF Treaty
in Washington, the first agreement to

50
00:03:35,558 --> 00:03:38,801
eliminate a whole class
of nuclear missiles.

51
00:03:38,851 --> 00:03:43,890
The arms race, which was swallowing
Soviet resources, began to slow.

52
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:47,433
In the West, Gorbachev became a celebrity.

53
00:03:47,483 --> 00:03:53,447
But reform has a way of releasing forces
that its author cannot control, and inside

54
00:03:53,497 --> 00:03:57,070
the Soviet Union, the
results were very different.

55
00:03:57,620 --> 00:04:00,505
The Soviet economy was
planned by officials.

56
00:04:00,555 --> 00:04:06,043
Prices were fixed, factories received
quotas, and a huge share of resources went to

57
00:04:06,093 --> 00:04:06,910
the military.

58
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:12,447
It could build rockets and tanks, but it
struggled to make good consumer goods, and

59
00:04:12,497 --> 00:04:16,450
it fell further behind the West
in computers and technology.

60
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,295
One thing held it together: oil.

61
00:04:19,345 --> 00:04:25,304
Oil and gas brought in most of the hard
currency the country used to buy grain and

62
00:04:25,354 --> 00:04:26,917
machinery from abroad.

63
00:04:26,967 --> 00:04:32,853
Then, in nineteen eighty-six, the price
of oil collapsed, from about twenty-seven

64
00:04:32,903 --> 00:04:36,590
dollars a barrel the year
before to about fourteen.

65
00:04:37,140 --> 00:04:41,608
The country's income shrank just
as it was trying to reform.

66
00:04:41,658 --> 00:04:47,481
Shortages grew worse, the budget deficit
widened, and the new rules often made

67
00:04:47,531 --> 00:04:49,739
things more chaotic, not less.

68
00:04:49,789 --> 00:04:55,010
In some cities, ration coupons returned
for goods like sugar and soap.

69
00:04:55,560 --> 00:05:00,640
By the end of the decade, empty
shelves had become part of daily life.

70
00:05:00,690 --> 00:05:03,352
And people could now say so out loud.

71
00:05:03,402 --> 00:05:06,870
Openness had given them
the freedom to complain.

72
00:05:07,420 --> 00:05:10,441
There was also a war that would not end.

73
00:05:10,491 --> 00:05:16,661
In December nineteen seventy-nine, Soviet
troops entered Afghanistan to prop up a

74
00:05:16,711 --> 00:05:20,730
friendly government. They
expected a short operation.

75
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:27,075
Instead, they met a determined insurgency,
backed with weapons and money from

76
00:05:27,125 --> 00:05:31,022
abroad, in mountains where
tanks were of little use.

77
00:05:31,072 --> 00:05:33,603
The war dragged on for nine years.

78
00:05:33,653 --> 00:05:39,675
Roughly fifteen thousand Soviet soldiers
were killed, and Afghan casualties were

79
00:05:39,725 --> 00:05:40,510
far higher.

80
00:05:41,060 --> 00:05:43,553
Gorbachev called it a bleeding wound.

81
00:05:43,603 --> 00:05:49,256
The last Soviet troops crossed the border
in February nineteen eighty-nine, without

82
00:05:49,306 --> 00:05:53,036
anything resembling the
victory they had been promised.

83
00:05:53,086 --> 00:05:57,090
The war had cost lives, money
and, above all, public trust.

84
00:05:57,640 --> 00:06:03,491
On the twenty-sixth of April nineteen
eighty-six, during a safety test, reactor

85
00:06:03,541 --> 00:06:08,644
number four at the Chernobyl nuclear
power plant in Ukraine exploded.

86
00:06:08,694 --> 00:06:14,470
A fire burned for days and sent radioactive
material high into the atmosphere.

87
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:18,621
For two days the Soviet public
was told almost nothing.

88
00:06:18,671 --> 00:06:22,405
It was radiation alarms in
Sweden that alerted the world.

89
00:06:22,455 --> 00:06:27,650
Tens of thousands of people were evacuated
from the nearby town of Pripyat, and

90
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:31,169
hundreds of thousands more
were eventually resettled.

91
00:06:31,219 --> 00:06:36,347
Firefighters, soldiers and miners worked
on the site, many of them paying with

92
00:06:36,397 --> 00:06:37,210
their health.

93
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:43,325
Chernobyl showed how much the system hid,
and how little citizens could trust it.

94
00:06:43,375 --> 00:06:48,802
Gorbachev himself later suggested that
it may have been the real turning point,

95
00:06:48,852 --> 00:06:50,257
more than any reform.

96
00:06:50,307 --> 00:06:55,110
The cleanup cost the state enormous
sums at the worst possible moment.

97
00:06:55,660 --> 00:06:58,856
The first cracks appeared in the Caucasus.

98
00:06:58,906 --> 00:07:05,116
In February nineteen eighty-eight, Armenians
in Nagorno-Karabakh, a region inside

99
00:07:05,166 --> 00:07:11,608
Azerbaijan, demanded to join Armenia, and
violence between the two peoples followed.

100
00:07:11,658 --> 00:07:16,090
A state built to deny such
conflicts could not resolve it.

101
00:07:16,640 --> 00:07:22,344
As glasnost spread, people across the
fifteen republics began to talk about what

102
00:07:22,394 --> 00:07:26,227
had long been suppressed:
language, history, identity.

103
00:07:26,277 --> 00:07:32,053
In the Baltic states, activists revealed
the secret deal of nineteen thirty-nine,

104
00:07:32,103 --> 00:07:37,950
the Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact, that had led
to their annexation by the Soviet Union.

105
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:44,224
On the twenty-third of August nineteen
eighty-nine, the fiftieth anniversary of

106
00:07:44,274 --> 00:07:50,145
that pact, about two million people joined
hands in a human chain across Estonia,

107
00:07:50,195 --> 00:07:55,627
Latvia and Lithuania, roughly six
hundred and seventy-five kilometres long.

108
00:07:55,677 --> 00:08:00,890
It was called the Baltic Way. It was
peaceful, and impossible to ignore.

109
00:08:01,440 --> 00:08:04,237
Elsewhere, protest was met with force.

110
00:08:04,287 --> 00:08:09,932
Soviet troops killed demonstrators in
Tbilisi, in Georgia, in April nineteen

111
00:08:09,982 --> 00:08:14,427
eighty-nine, and in Vilnius, in
January nineteen ninety-one.

112
00:08:14,477 --> 00:08:19,447
Each time, Gorbachev's reputation
suffered, and the movements grew.

113
00:08:19,497 --> 00:08:25,666
Nationalism, once hidden under one flag,
was becoming the strongest political force

114
00:08:25,716 --> 00:08:26,790
in the country.

115
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:30,129
Meanwhile, Eastern Europe was watching.

116
00:08:30,179 --> 00:08:35,735
Gorbachev had made it clear that Moscow
would no longer send tanks to prop up

117
00:08:35,785 --> 00:08:37,337
unpopular governments.

118
00:08:37,387 --> 00:08:43,162
In Poland, the trade union Solidarity won
partly free elections in June nineteen

119
00:08:43,212 --> 00:08:47,530
eighty-nine. In Hungary, the
border with Austria was opened.

120
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:53,658
Then, on the ninth of November nineteen
eighty-nine, East Germany announced that

121
00:08:53,708 --> 00:08:56,261
its citizens could cross to the West.

122
00:08:56,311 --> 00:09:01,889
Crowds gathered at the Berlin Wall, the
guards stepped aside, and people climbed

123
00:09:01,939 --> 00:09:04,984
it, danced on it, and
began to tear it down.

124
00:09:05,034 --> 00:09:10,190
The wall that had divided Europe for
twenty-eight years was suddenly open.

125
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:16,822
Within weeks, communist governments fell
in Czechoslovakia and Bulgaria, and by

126
00:09:16,872 --> 00:09:18,530
Christmas, in Romania.

127
00:09:18,580 --> 00:09:24,507
The order that Soviet power had held in
place for forty years came apart in a

128
00:09:24,557 --> 00:09:27,690
single year, almost without Soviet force.

129
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:30,700
At home, politics had changed too.

130
00:09:30,750 --> 00:09:36,679
In March nineteen ninety, the Communist
Party gave up its constitutional monopoly

131
00:09:36,729 --> 00:09:42,732
on power, and Gorbachev became the first,
and only, president of the Soviet Union.

132
00:09:42,782 --> 00:09:44,430
But a rival was rising.

133
00:09:44,980 --> 00:09:50,911
Boris Yeltsin, a former party chief in
Moscow, had been pushed out by Gorbachev,

134
00:09:50,961 --> 00:09:54,201
and returned as a champion
of Russia itself.

135
00:09:54,251 --> 00:09:58,463
In June nineteen ninety, Russia
declared its sovereignty.

136
00:09:58,513 --> 00:10:03,696
A year later, Yeltsin was elected
President of Russia by popular vote.

137
00:10:03,746 --> 00:10:08,930
Now Moscow had two centres of power:
the Soviet Union's, and Russia's.

138
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:13,548
Gorbachev tried one more way
to hold the country together.

139
00:10:13,598 --> 00:10:19,299
In a referendum in March nineteen ninety-one,
about three out of four voters said

140
00:10:19,349 --> 00:10:23,986
yes to a renewed union, though six
republics refused to take part.

141
00:10:24,036 --> 00:10:29,524
He began negotiating a new Union Treaty
that would give the republics far more

142
00:10:29,574 --> 00:10:29,950
power.

143
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:36,024
The new treaty was due to be signed on the
twentieth of August nineteen ninety-one.

144
00:10:36,074 --> 00:10:41,329
Hardliners in the government, the army
and the KGB feared that it would end the

145
00:10:41,379 --> 00:10:45,090
Soviet Union as they knew it.
They decided to act first.

146
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:51,168
On the morning of the nineteenth, Soviet
radio announced that Gorbachev was ill,

147
00:10:51,218 --> 00:10:54,515
and that an emergency
committee had taken power.

148
00:10:54,565 --> 00:10:59,327
In fact, he was being held under
guard at his holiday home in Crimea.

149
00:10:59,377 --> 00:11:01,070
Tanks rolled into Moscow.

150
00:11:01,620 --> 00:11:05,356
But the plotters hesitated.
They did not arrest Yeltsin.

151
00:11:05,406 --> 00:11:10,697
He went to the Russian parliament building,
climbed onto a tank outside it, and

152
00:11:10,747 --> 00:11:12,522
called on people to resist.

153
00:11:12,572 --> 00:11:16,578
Thousands gathered to defend
the building through the night.

154
00:11:16,628 --> 00:11:20,770
Three people died in clashes,
but the army would not storm it.

155
00:11:21,320 --> 00:11:23,995
Within three days the coup collapsed.

156
00:11:24,045 --> 00:11:28,560
The plotters were arrested, and
Gorbachev flew back to Moscow.

157
00:11:28,610 --> 00:11:31,432
But he returned to a different country.

158
00:11:31,482 --> 00:11:34,967
His authority was gone,
and Yeltsin's had grown.

159
00:11:35,017 --> 00:11:41,005
Within days, Gorbachev resigned as head of
the Communist Party, and its activities

160
00:11:41,055 --> 00:11:42,110
were suspended.

161
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:46,406
The coup had finished off
what it meant to save.

162
00:11:46,456 --> 00:11:50,282
One republic after another
declared independence:

163
00:11:50,332 --> 00:11:55,027
Ukraine, Belarus, Moldova,
Azerbaijan, Uzbekistan, and more.

164
00:11:55,077 --> 00:11:58,902
Moscow recognised the
Baltic states in September.

165
00:11:58,952 --> 00:12:05,071
And in a referendum on the first of
December, about nine out of ten Ukrainians

166
00:12:05,121 --> 00:12:06,890
voted for independence.

167
00:12:07,440 --> 00:12:10,399
Without Ukraine, a
union made little sense.

168
00:12:10,449 --> 00:12:16,066
On the eighth of December nineteen
ninety-one, the leaders of Russia, Ukraine and

169
00:12:16,116 --> 00:12:21,873
Belarus met at a hunting lodge in a forest
in Belarus, and declared that the Soviet

170
00:12:21,923 --> 00:12:23,692
Union had ceased to exist.

171
00:12:23,742 --> 00:12:28,590
They created a looser Commonwealth
of Independent States in its place.

172
00:12:29,140 --> 00:12:34,829
Two weeks later, in Alma-Ata, eleven
republics agreed to join the Commonwealth.

173
00:12:34,879 --> 00:12:40,859
Gorbachev was still the president of the
Soviet Union, but there was almost nothing

174
00:12:40,909 --> 00:12:42,530
left for him to govern.

175
00:12:43,080 --> 00:12:48,631
On the twenty-fifth of December, Gorbachev
went on television and announced his

176
00:12:48,681 --> 00:12:49,481
resignation.

177
00:12:49,531 --> 00:12:55,294
He said he had opposed the breakup of the
country, but that events had gone beyond

178
00:12:55,344 --> 00:12:58,910
his control. He spoke of
apprehension, and of hope.

179
00:12:59,460 --> 00:13:05,199
That evening, the red flag of the Soviet
Union was lowered over the Kremlin for the

180
00:13:05,249 --> 00:13:10,221
last time, and the white, blue and
red flag of Russia rose in its place.

181
00:13:10,271 --> 00:13:15,732
The next day, the upper chamber of the
Soviet parliament voted the Union out of

182
00:13:15,782 --> 00:13:19,010
existence. After sixty-nine
years, it was over.

183
00:13:19,560 --> 00:13:24,455
Why did it happen? Historians still
argue. Some point to the economy:

184
00:13:24,505 --> 00:13:30,333
a system that could not keep up with the
West, and could not afford the arms race.

185
00:13:30,383 --> 00:13:36,138
Others stress the damage done by Afghanistan
and Chernobyl, which wore away trust

186
00:13:36,188 --> 00:13:37,070
in the state.

187
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:43,718
Some emphasise nationalism, the pull of
fifteen peoples, many of whom wanted to run

188
00:13:43,768 --> 00:13:48,310
their own affairs. Others point
to the choices of individuals:

189
00:13:48,360 --> 00:13:54,310
Gorbachev's reforms, which weakened the
old order before a new one was ready, and

190
00:13:54,360 --> 00:13:59,050
Yeltsin's drive for power. Few
believe there was a single cause.

191
00:13:59,600 --> 00:14:03,394
What nearly everyone agrees
on is how quickly it came.

192
00:14:03,444 --> 00:14:09,302
Only a few years earlier, almost no one,
in Moscow or in the West, had expected the

193
00:14:09,352 --> 00:14:13,430
Soviet Union to vanish in the
lifetime of its last leader.

194
00:14:13,980 --> 00:14:17,068
Fifteen new countries
emerged from the Union.

195
00:14:17,118 --> 00:14:22,716
Russia took the Soviet seat at the United
Nations and inherited most of its armed

196
00:14:22,766 --> 00:14:23,204
forces.

197
00:14:23,254 --> 00:14:28,643
Ukraine, Belarus and Kazakhstan later
agreed to give up the nuclear weapons on

198
00:14:28,693 --> 00:14:29,410
their soil.

199
00:14:29,960 --> 00:14:35,432
For millions of people, the nineteen-nineties
brought hardship, inflation and

200
00:14:35,482 --> 00:14:38,085
uncertainty, along with new freedoms.

201
00:14:38,135 --> 00:14:44,037
The effects of that period, and of the
Soviet Union's end, still shape the politics

202
00:14:44,087 --> 00:14:45,830
of Europe and Asia today.

203
00:14:46,380 --> 00:14:49,370
The flag came down in a single evening.

204
00:14:49,420 --> 00:14:55,839
But the questions it left behind, about
power, freedom and the price of change, are

205
00:14:55,889 --> 00:14:56,930
still with us.
